      "text": "$10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 2$ does not work because we cannot have a number with a zero in the hundreds place.  We are left with \\[\n10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 1 = 900, \n\\] for $A.$  Furthermore,  all of these are multiples of five.  We must divide by 10 to get \\[\n\\frac{900}{10} = 90. \n\\] Thus, $A+B = 90 + 90 = \\boxed{180}.$\nWe could do a similar approach with the second part of the problem.\n\nIf we add ones hundreds place, this would give us $4 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 2$ options.  This simplifies to \\[\n4 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 100 \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 200 = \\boxed{800}.\n\\].  When we add, we get $A+B = 800 + 90 = \\boxed{890}.$.",
